From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 17:34:18 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] vkr: read the object type under the mutex that guards object
 destroy

vkr_cs_decoder_lookup_object() releases dec->object_mutex and only then reads
obj->type:

    mtx_lock(dec->object_mutex);
    const struct hash_entry *entry = _mesa_hash_table_search(..., &id);
    obj = likely(entry) ? entry->data : NULL;
    mtx_unlock(dec->object_mutex);
    if (unlikely(!obj || obj->type != type)) {

vkr_context_free_object() runs under that same mutex, and with more than one
ring each ring has its own dispatch thread (vkr_ring.c:344 -> :263 -> :301), so
the type read races a concurrent destroy of the very object just looked up. The
type is what decides whether the command is rejected as a fatal stream error, so
a torn read here turns a healthy command into a dead context.

Move the comparison inside the critical section and keep only the logging tail
outside it.

This narrows the window rather than closing it: the caller goes on to use obj
for the rest of the command with no lock held, which cannot be fixed without
refcounting objects and is out of scope for a patch this size.

Found while investigating DomA's SurfaceFlinger Venus context dying with
"object 122 has type 19, not 9" on RPi4. It is NOT established that this is that
defect's cause -- under VN_PERF=no_multi_ring there is only one dispatch thread
and the failure still occurs, and the secondary rings that multi-ring adds carry
only vkCreatePipelines/vkGetPipelineCacheData (Mesa's vn_pipeline.c:458-481), so
nothing on them destroys a buffer or an image. Fixed because the code is wrong
on its own terms, not because it closes the case.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/src/venus/vkr_cs.h
+++ b/src/venus/vkr_cs.h
@@ -357,10 +357,20 @@
    const struct hash_entry *entry =
       _mesa_hash_table_search((struct hash_table *)dec->object_table, &id);
    obj = likely(entry) ? entry->data : NULL;
+   /* obj->type has to be read under the same lock vkr_context_free_object() is
+    * called under; releasing first leaves both the type read and the pointer
+    * handed back to the caller racing an object destroy on another ring
+    * thread. Only the logging tail is kept outside the critical section.
+    *
+    * This narrows the window rather than closing it: the caller still uses obj
+    * after the unlock, which cannot be fixed without refcounting objects.
+    */
+   const bool bad = unlikely(!obj || obj->type != type);
+   const VkObjectType got = obj ? obj->type : VK_OBJECT_TYPE_UNKNOWN;
    mtx_unlock(dec->object_mutex);
-   if (unlikely(!obj || obj->type != type)) {
+   if (bad) {
       if (obj)
-         vkr_log("object %" PRIu64 " has type %d, not %d", id, obj->type, type);
+         vkr_log("object %" PRIu64 " has type %d, not %d", id, got, type);
       else
          vkr_log("failed to look up object %" PRIu64 " of type %d", id, type);
       vkr_cs_decoder_set_fatal(dec);
